f the histogram you can have  nucleated rbcs you can have giant  platelets you can have platelet clamps  you can have even malarial parasites  they all can go in there and you know  what happens up until the dx800 there's  nothing you can do you can just alert  the customer there is a problem and they  have to do the work of figuring out what  happened and cleaning it up  now  thanks to the xh800 and new histograms  we are being able to isolate these  different interfering particles so here  we have giant platelets and as you can  see they can be as large as a white cell  and that's why they're causing cell  interference well  in this histogram here which is a unique  the ex it's one of the new histograms  let's discriminate new histograms from  traditional histogram the traditional  one is the one that every instrument had  before the dx800 is typically one for  size and one for  granularity so we have we had on the lh  analyzers volume versus laser light  scatter alcohol might have forward  fluorescence versus side fluorescence  right in this traditional histogram they  may all fall in the same area but now we  have a new histogram here which is  created by low angle light scatter  against actual light loss and look at  the position of the giant platelets in  this histogram  and let's look at the position of  platelet clamps which is another  abnormality causing exactly this problem  cell interference  in this new histogram they are in a  completely different area  at the same time nucleated red blood  cells also are in a completely different  area as you can see here  therefore we can now count nucleated  reboot cells with much better accuracy  and therefore these cells are not  causing interferences anymore and  they've nucleated in their white cell  counting this means number one that the  accounts being reported by the  instrument are more reliable and the  likelihood of reporting out a a false  leukocytosis because nucleated rats  ended up being counted as whites is  tremendously decreased because of this  optimal detection that we have now  thanks to this new histogram  and and that has been reported out in  the literature but at the same time it  also is allowing us to  to  even if you had an instrument that could  have detected that there was an  instrument uh an interference  now we don't have to work to clean this  interference anymore because the  instrument is already doing that for you  without any additional workload the same  also for parasitized rbcs here you have  a  blood cells with a  malaria parasites in fact in them they  also can cause cell interference but  they also have a completely different  position in the histogram as you can see  here so here we have  on the upper side  we have um one patient and on the bottom  you have a second patient and on the  left you have these patients uh  histograms at the time when they were  diagnosed with malaria and then in the  center during treatment and on the right  to have after treatment and if you look  where the arrows are they are showing is  very small green population so this is  the position of malarially infected  abysses  in this histogram which is completely  different from the position of anab  nucleotide resist which is completely  different from the position of  giant platelets and completely different  from the position of data clamps so now  we can discriminate them all and clean  it for you  another very nice example of desk how  this technology is helping patients is  uh on the reticulocyte counting so let's  go back into how this works so for  example when we are counting writings we  must understand that  inside the hematological analyzer we  don't have only one instrument we  actually have many instruments inside  the same box we call these instruments  chambers so you have a white cell  chamber counting whites and you have a  red cell chamber counting reds and they  have a radical chamber counting writings  and the treatment of blood in different  chambers is different so for the ritik  chamber  we have to actually clean the hemoglobin  of the cells when we're going to  counteratix why because the writings are  differentiated from the reds because  they have remnants of rna in the  cytoplasm and we have to stain these  remnants of rna we use a dye called  supravital dye pneumonia in blue and we  stain the rna remnants and then when we  run this blood in the laser light  scatter the remnants of rna  have caused the stained once cause a  high laser light scatter now in order to  stain them and be able to discriminate  that from other cells we have to  completely clear the hemoglobin from the  cell  and we do that by adding a very light  acid to the blood um this process of  cleaning hemoglobin we call this the  hemoglobinization or the better yet we  have a term which is and ghosting cells  right  we have here on the right you can see  reticulocytes with the little blue dots  in them there's this reticuline this is  basically rna stain the pneumatic bloom  and then you have the pale blue cells  which are ghosted rbcs  now of course when i run this sample on  the right in the laser  your  reticulocytes that have these little  blue dots are gonna have a higher laser  light scatter and that's how we measure  them  but but the problem is  if the ghosting process  failed okay if the cleaning of  hemoglobin did not work well  um i'm gonna end up with cells that are  engosted so they are not reticulocytes  but they have remnants of cytoplasm  hemoglobin in the cytoplasm and  therefore they also have a high laser  right scatter and they end up going  exactly the position of the  reticulocytes  also if you have inclusions on your rbcs  you may have proper harmony  pop and hybrid body bodies you may have  height bodies these are all inclusions  that mean that when you run the cells on  the laser they will also have a high  laser light scatter the result is that  if you go in the literature and you see  if you type in on pubmed pseudo  reticulocytosis  you observe a tremendous number of  papers describing many conditions that  give you a falsely elevated reticulocyte  count as reported by hematological  instruments why is that because the  instruments are basically counting cells  with high laser light scatter and until  the exhibit hundred they had no way of  telling that these cells are not  reticulocytes that they are an unghosted  rbc  now we have the dx800 with multiple  angles of light scatter so let's look at  these four images here  uh the e and don't look at the visuals  okay here we have both density plots and  surface plots  that doesn't matter you can either you  can transform in  the decimal plot here in a surface part  that's not a question the question is  the parameter that is being used  and for these four images the one on the  right the one that is a density plot you  see the parameter is volume versus  light scatter  this is the traditional histogram  and you can see in purple the position  where the reticulocytes go okay  now on the left in the surface plots the  parameters being used to create this  histogram they are not volume and laser  light scatter they are actual light loss  which is one angle flat scatter against  upper middle angular scatter which is  another end of light scatter so this is  basically one of the new  histograms that are unique to the dx800  technology so note that you are seeing a  population of yellow  cells yellow dots that you can see  in a clearly different position  from the purple dots  they are different in the new histogram  but when you go to the traditional  histogram the volume laser light scatter  you can see that they are exactly in the  area where you expect reticulocytes to  be  now thanks to this having this new  histogram we now can isolate  true reticulocytes that are here dotted  in purple and you can see them here  from this unghosted cells that are  causing interference and because we can  isolate them we can remove them  from the counting of reticulocytes and  therefore  in this technology we are not having  cellular reticulocytosis we are  reporting an accurate rhythmic count  even in the presence of this common  interference substances and and that is  basically telling the laboratory that  you have to go to the microscope fewer  times you have less workload and most  importantly importantly you have an  accurate result and it will not report  out to a clinician a highly elevated  ready count  when the patient does not have that  and and this is really critical  so you have a reliable count in the  process of abnormalities  so again basically the key message i  think is that laboratory hematology is  more than counting cells  um and and the major limitation of  modern day laboratory hematology is not  lack of parameters it's not lack of new  counts of cells especially if you have  new accounts that are not are not  providing an improved performance  demonstrated by rockers the major  limitation of modern laboratory  hematology is the fact that we are no  longer appreciating the morphology  and and we need to change the game and  we have now an instrument that is  looking at the cells for you at the same  time that it is counting them and we  just need to use this uh technology to  improve our patient care and not allow  and without having an increase in  workload and labor costs  thank you so much and i'll i'll be happy  to take any questions thank you  you dr chavez  if you have questions please feel free  to submit them at this time  okay there's there are no questions in  our queue  so that will conclude our presentation  thank you for attending and enjoy the  rest of the conference  you
 

